; BT-9. NIGHT EDGE -- CYAN. Program decision D10.
;
; Assigned by the geometry packets to the EDGE meshes/rims they author (cyan section obstacle rims).
; A normal matte surface by day; an emissive rim once `bt_darkness` rises. It EMITS and never
; LIGHTS -- see `night_edge.gdshader` header for why that distinction is the whole design, and
; for why the rim is a Fresnel band rather than a glowing face.
;
; TWO OVERRIDES ONLY: the body colour and the edge colour. Everything about HOW the edge
; behaves -- rim tightness, the darkness onset, the looking-straight-down correction -- lives in
; the shader, so all three night-edge materials behave identically and a tuning pass is one
; file rather than three that drift apart.
[gd_resource type="ShaderMaterial" load_steps=2 format=3]

[ext_resource type="Shader" path="res://resources/shaders/night_edge.gdshader" id="1_edge"]

[resource]
render_priority = 0
shader = ExtResource("1_edge")
; The DAYLIGHT body: the shared mid gray from the section palette (neutral_gray.tres colour).
; The rims are ordinary neutral geometry in daylight -- Talon rule "most geometry stays neutral
; gray/white" applies to them too, and D10 aids are a NIGHT feature, not permanent decoration.
shader_parameter/albedo = Color(0.227, 0.227, 0.23, 1)
; The NIGHT edge: the section goal colour exactly (cyan_goal.tres). Using the goal colour rather than a
; second tone is deliberate -- at night the lit edge becomes the section identity marker, which
; is the same job the goal colour does by day, so wayfinding does not change vocabulary at dusk.
shader_parameter/edge_color = Color(0.119, 0.826, 0.850, 1)
